    VALUES (subj, tid, 'NUMERICAL', 'An initially uncharged 1 mF capacitor has the current shown in the figure. What is the voltage across it at t = 2 ms?', 'An initially uncharged 1 mF capacitor has the current shown in the figure. What is the voltage across it at t = 2 ms?

![Figure](fig:q-ese2024-60)
', '**Idea used:** change in capacitor voltage = (area under the current graph) / C (see *The capacitor → Voltage from current*).

1. From 0 to 2 ms the current rises in a straight line from 0 to 100 mA. The area is a triangle:
   $q = \tfrac12\times(2\times10^{-3})\times(100\times10^{-3}) = 1\times10^{-4}$ C.
2. $v(2\ \text{ms}) = 0 + \dfrac{q}{C} = \dfrac{10^{-4}}{1\times10^{-3}} = 0.1$ V $= 100$ mV.

Trap: using the rectangle $100\ \text{mA}\times2\ \text{ms}$ gives 200 mV, which is wrong; the current was not 100 mA all the time.

**Without a calculator:** Use units that cancel: mA × ms = µC, and µC ÷ mF = mV. Area $= \tfrac12 \times 2 \times 100 = 100$ µC, divided by 1 mF $= 100$ mV.

**Answer: (B) 100 mV.**
