    VALUES (subj, tid, 'NUMERICAL', 'A current shown in Fig. 4 passes through a pure inductance of 3 mH. The instantaneous power in watt during 0 < t < 2 ms is', 'A current shown in Fig. 4 passes through a pure inductance of 3 mH. The instantaneous power in watt during 0 < t < 2 ms is

![Fig. 4](fig:q-aegcl2023-83)
', '**Idea used:** $v = L\,di/dt$ and $p = vi$ (see *The inductor → Power and energy in an inductor*).

1. From 0 to 2 ms the current rises linearly from 0 to 10 A: $i = \dfrac{10}{2\times10^{-3}}\,t = 5000\,t$ A.
2. $v = L\,\dfrac{di}{dt} = 3\times10^{-3}\times5000 = 15$ V (constant during this interval).
3. $p = vi = 15\times5000\,t = 75\,000\,t$ W.

The power grows with time because the current grows while the voltage stays constant: the inductor is storing energy. (Check: energy at 2 ms $= \tfrac12\times3\times10^{-3}\times10^2 = 0.15$ J, and $\int_0^{0.002}75000\,t\,dt = 37500\times4\times10^{-6} = 0.15$ J ✔.)

**Without a calculator:** Slope $= 10$ A in 2 ms $= 5000$ A/s. $v = 3\ \text{mH} \times 5000 = 15$ V. Then $15 \times 5000 = 75000$. All options have the form $k\,t$, so only the number matters.

**Answer: (B) 75000 t.**
', 'READY', 'MCQ', NULL, NULL, 2, 'UNVERIFIED', 'aegcl-2023-83', 'PYQ') RETURNING id INTO qid;
